// SYNTAX TEST "source.kama" "spellings the COMPILER REJECTS — the grammar must colour them illegal"
//
// The inverse fixture. `tools/check-syntax.sh` asserts that `kama check` FAILS on this file, and that
// every construct below carries an `invalid.illegal.*` scope. Colouring rejected code as valid is the
// exact defect class this whole audit exists to close: before it, the grammar highlighted `42u32` as a
// number (the suffix is `u?i…`, so it is `42ui32`), `\e` and `\x41` as escapes, `'abc'` and `''` as char
// literals, and let `@"say ""hi"""` silently mis-colour the rest of its line.
//
// Nothing here needs to be reachable or type-correct — it needs to be REJECTED.

fn int32 main() {
    string e1 = "\e";
    string e2 = "\x41";
    string e3 = "\q";
    string e4 = "\u{1234567}";
    char c1 = 'abc';
    char c2 = '';
    int32 n1 = 42u32;
    int32 n2 = 42f32;
    int32 n4 = 0b1010;
    return 0;
}
